     1	# The DeFi State-Transition Atlas — v1.0
     2	
     3	*Informally, and by provenance, "the periodic table of DeFi". The nickname is
     4	kept because it is the term readers arrive with. The formal name is used
     5	everywhere the difference matters, because there is no periodic law here — see
     6	§19.*
     7	
     8	**What it is:** a typed vocabulary for describing what a DeFi protocol
     9	*mechanically does*, plus composition rules that say which combinations are
    10	required, which are hazardous, and which have never been built.
    11	
    12	**Contents:** 48 core elements · 10 candidates · 10 provisional · 16 groups ·
    13	5 dependency strata · 4 typed bond classes · 29 required-bond laws · 19 hazard
    14	rules · an atomicity phase diagram · four overlays.
    15	
    16	**Status:** v1.0, revised after a blinded council review across eight lanes and
    17	three provider families, in which every lane returned `changes_requested`
    18	(84 findings). What changed and why is in Appendix C.
    19	Unresolved dissent is preserved in §20.4, not averaged away.
    20	
    21	---
    22	
    23	## 1. Quickstart
    24	
    25	If you read nothing else, read this page.
    26	
    27	**The object.** A protocol is a *molecule*. It decomposes into *elements* —
    28	recurring financial state transitions — joined by *bonds* of four types, valid
    29	only inside stated *reaction conditions*, and leaving *residue* that the
    30	vocabulary cannot express. Residue is reported, never hidden.
    31	
    32	**One formula, read aloud:**
    33	
    34	```
    35	Across@2026
    36	  elements:   In + Rf + Of + Xm{trust_domain=optimistic} + Xf{burn-mint}
    37	  bonds:      In —n→ filler [enables: adverse_selection]
    38	              Of —e→ Bs{trigger=non-fill}
    39	              Xm —t→ external-validator-set
    40	  conditions: cross-ecosystem atomicity; filler inventory depth; dispute window
    41	  residue:    filler inventory economics; relayer concentration
    42	```
    43	
    44	Read: *users sign intents; fillers quote and advance destination value before
    45	finality; an optimistic verifier settles the reimbursement; value moves
    46	burn-and-mint. The filler sees the order before it settles, which is worth
    47	money to them. The advance is underwritten by bonded capital.*
    48	
    49	**What the table then tells you, mechanically:** `Of` requires a timeout and a
    50	loss allocator (law L19). `Xm` cannot be written bare — a verifier's trust
    51	domain changes which hazard rules apply (§8.2). `In` obliges you to declare who
    52	observes the order (law L24). Miss any of these and the formula is ill-formed,
    53	which is the point: ill-formedness is where the risk was hiding.
    54	
    55	**Where to go next.** To describe a protocol → §18.1. To screen a design →
    56	§18.2. To diagnose an incident → §18.3. To find gaps → §18.4. To learn the
    57	notation → §12. A complete worked example, start to finish, is Appendix A.
    58	
    59	**Three things this table will not do for you.** It will not catch an
    60	implementation bug (§17.3). It will not predict a key compromise, and by 2025
    61	dollars that is where the losses are (§17.4). It will not describe a legal
    62	instrument to a regulator (§19).
    63	
    64	---
    65	
    66	## 2. The object model
    67	
    68	Eight constructs, plus laws, conditions and overlays defined in §13–§17. (v0.1
    69	claimed "nine constructs and everything else is one of these"; that was false,
    70	and Catalyst has since been removed from the list — see §11.)
    71	
    72	| Construct | Definition | Test |
    73	|---|---|---|
    74	| **Element** | An independently recurring, role-substitutable financial state-transition mechanism with a distinct failure signature | The admission test, §3 |
    75	| **Group** | A family of elements that can occupy the same role boundary and leave the protocol recognizably the same kind of system | Substitution, §5 |
    76	| **Stratum (period)** | The category of prerequisite an element minimally needs to exist | §6 |
    77	| **Isotope** | A variant, continuous or discrete, that preserves the state-transition semantics **and the failure family** | §7 |
    78	| **Discriminator** | A mandatory annotation whose values select different hazard rules | §8.2 |
    79	| **Bond** | A typed required relationship: interface, economic, trust, informational | §9 |
    80	| **Valence** | The typed vector of bonds an element requires | §10 |
    81	| **Molecule** | A deployed protocol-version: elements + parameters + typed bonds + reaction conditions + residue | §12 |
    82	| **Residue** | What a decomposition leaves unexpressed, per molecule | §12.4 |
    83	
    84	**A note on the word "residue".** v0.1 used it for two different things. In
    85	v1.0 *residue* is only the per-molecule remainder. The corpus-level statistic —
    86	what share of incidents the table fails to explain — is called the
    87	**unexplained-incident share** (§17.4).
    88	
    89	---
    90	
    91	## 3. The admission test
    92	
    93	An entity is an **element** if all of the following hold. Where v0.1 stated a
    94	seven-way conjunction and then admitted elements that failed it, v1.0 separates
    95	what is *necessary* from what is *evidence of maturity*.
    96	
    97	### 3.1 Necessary criteria
    98	
    99	| Test | Requirement |
   100	|---|---|
   101	| **F** — functional irreducibility | No decomposition into two independently recurring mechanisms reproduces the function without losing its defining state transition |
   102	| **R-struct** — structural separability | The mechanism is separable in principle: its state and transition can be stated without reference to the protocol that hosts it |
   103	| **S** — role-preserving substitutability | A group sibling can replace it at the same role boundary and the application stays the same protocol class |
   104	| **D** — distinct failure signature | It creates at least one failure mode not fully attributable to another element |
   105	| **B** — state-transition boundary | It changes claims, obligations, allocation, valuation or settlement; **or** it changes which claim-changing transitions are reachable, and does so as persistent, independently parameterised policy state |
   106	| **P** — model portability | Its semantics are statable over an abstract settlement scope, without reference to a specific VM's call, storage, gas or ordering model |
   107	| **O** — observability | Identifiable in documentation, interfaces, state variables, events or auditable execution |
   108	
   109	### 3.2 R-emp — a graded attribute, not a gate
   110	
   111	**R-emp** records observed independent recurrence: named unrelated teams and
   112	named independent code lineages. Forks count once.
   113	
   114	R-emp determines *status*, not membership:
   115	
   116	| Status | R-emp | Meaning |
   117	|---|---|---|
   118	| **core** | ≥ 3 unrelated teams and ≥ 2 lineages, named | Safe to rely on in analysis |
   119	| **candidate** | R-struct met, R-emp short or a gate pending | Usable in formulas, flagged |
   120	| **provisional** | A necessary criterion is genuinely contested | Not usable in a formula without a note |
   121	| **degenerate limit** | A boundary case of another element whose **failure family differs** | Written as a limit, not an isotope |
   122	
   123	**Why this changed.** v0.1 made recurrence necessary, which conflated atomicity
   124	with diffusion and contradicted its own admission that a new primitive can ship
   125	next week — under that rule a genuinely irreducible new mechanism could not be
   126	an element until imitators arrived, and the whole P4 cohort carried an
   127	uncorrected recency bias. It also produced a live contradiction: `Rs` sat in the
   128	core set while the open register conceded its recurrence evidence was
   129	outstanding, and `Of` was admitted on two instantiations, while the same
   130	criterion was called a "hard gate" to demote constant sum. Splitting R fixes the
   131	asymmetry in the direction of honesty: fewer confident claims, none withdrawn.
   132	
   133	**Threshold sensitivity.** The 3-teams / 2-lineages line is a convention, not a
   134	derivation. It is the single largest determinant of the headline count. At 2/1,
   135	most current candidates promote and the count approaches 58. At 5/3, several
   136	P4 elements demote and the count falls toward 42. **Quote the count with its
   137	threshold or not at all.**
   138	
   139	### 3.3 Standing rejections
   140	
   141	Four categories are excluded by rule, not by enumeration.
   142	
   143	- **Standards are interface bonds, not elements.** ERC-4626, ERC-7540,
   144	  ERC-7683, ERC-7802 and the ERC-4337 account-abstraction family are sockets;
   145	  the mechanism plugged into them is the element. This applies to 4337 exactly

